WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.590
Let’s face it: trains kinda suck in America.

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:08.390
They’re slow, expensive, and just don’t
exist in many parts of the country.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:12.230
The simple reason for this is because the
US is so sparsely populated.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:17.050
Aside from here, here, and here, cities just
aren’t close enough together to make train

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.060
travel faster or cheaper than plane travel.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.359
When cities are within 200-300 miles of each
other, it’s often faster to take a train

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:29.120
from downtown to downtown rather than driving
to an airport, checking in, going through

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.470
security, flying, then driving downtown.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.899
However, as I mentioned, there definitely
are regions in the US with cities this distance

00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:38.800
away from each other, so why do trains still
suck?

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.320
The United States has the geography to support
trains in certain areas and yet a train from

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:49.190
DC to New York costs at least $49 dollars
and takes 3 hours and 29 minutes, only 30

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.480
minutes less than driving.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.980
A train from Rennes to Paris, France, a very
similar distance, costs 27 euros, the equivalent

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.800
of 30 dollars, and takes only 2 hours and
4 minutes.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.860
The US does have one high speed train, the
Acela express, but it costs at least $120

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.350
dollars for a ride from DC to New York and
still takes 2 hours and 50 minutes.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:14.710
Turkey, Poland, and Uzbekistan all have trains
that travel faster that America’s fastest

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:15.710
train.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:19.580
Alright, so understanding the whole issue
requires a bit of background knowledge.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.680
Back in their heyday, railroads in America
were… amazing.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:30.750
We built our first in 1826 and had a transcontinental
line by 1869—only 19 years after California

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.490
even became a state.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.049
Trains travelled almost everywhere and most
historians agree that the development of railroads

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:41.909
was an absolutely crucial catalyst to the
American industrialization period from 1843

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:43.310
to 1860.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:48.040
They even prompted the US to create one of
the world’s first standardized time systems

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.560
as discussed in an old video of mine which
you can find here.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.060
While passenger trains could sometimes be
profitable, freight services were where the

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.329
real money was so most rail companies basically
ran passenger services as a mobile advertisement

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:04.030
for their freight services to the executives
that would decide which company to ship goods

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:05.030
on.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:08.160
Trains were, after all, the most glamorous
and efficient way to travel.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.771
However, as cars became popular in the 30’s
and planes became popular in the 50’s, there

00:02:12.771 --> 00:02:17.110
was little purpose any more to set up passenger
services as advertisements to executives who

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:20.010
would be taking the more trendy car or plane
instead.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.890
At this point, the few profitable passenger
services only made money because of their

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.550
contracts with the US Postal Service.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.330
Most trains would have one car that served
as the railway post office—an office on

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.660
wheels where workers would sort letters en
route to the destination to save time.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:39.440
In the 1960’s, mail sorting was mechanized,
trucks and planes began to transport letters,

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.110
and railway post offices were discontinued.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.610
It became essentially impossible to make money
with a passenger railroad.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.600
By the end of the 1960’s the only thing
keeping the few passenger routes alive was

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.319
a legal obligation by the Interstate Commerce
Commission for the train companies to keep

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:54.819
running those routes.

00:02:54.819 --> 00:02:57.450
But then Amtrak came along.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:01.550
In 1970, President Nixon signed into law the
Rail Passenger Service Act which formed the

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:06.180
federally funded national rail company that
promised to save and make great again passenger

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.210
rail travel… except it didn’t.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:15.250
In the United States, a nation of 319 million
people, Amtrak operates a mere 300 train journeys

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:20.819
a day, while in France, a nation of 66 million
people, the Société Nationale des Chemins

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:28.200
de Fer Francais, also known as SNCF, operates
14,000 trips every single day, 800 of which

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.440
are high speed.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.519
One common criticism of Amtrak is its unreliability.

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:36.620
On average, only 72% of Amtrak’s trains
arrive on time.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:41.360
The California Zephyr route from Chicago to
San Francisco even arrived on time a paltry

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.840
31% in June of 2016.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.840
So what does Amtrak blame the delays on: freight
trains.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:54.599
You see, Amtrak only owns 730 miles of the
21,300 miles of track it operates on.

00:03:54.599 --> 00:03:59.250
On the California Zephyr route, Union Pacific
owns about half the track and BNSF owns the

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.250
other half.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.900
According to Amtrak, only 1.4% of all delays
on this route were their fault.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.689
The other 98.6 percent were reportedly the
fault of the rail companies who own the track.

00:04:09.689 --> 00:04:13.940
Union Pacific will naturally lend priority
to their own trains on their tracks instead

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:18.320
of Amtrak’s so Amtrak trains are often told
to wait to let a freight train pass.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.220
After all, it’s not like Amtrak can go and
use competing tracks so there’s little incentive

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.470
to give priority to passenger trains.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:27.199
Most rail operators in Europe don’t have
this problem.

00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:31.490
In France, for example, the national rail
company owns all the track so priority can

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.170
be given to passenger trains.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:39.450
Also, only 8% of freight in Europe is moved
by rail compared to 38% in the United States,

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.310
so there are far fewer freight trains congesting
the tracks.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:46.020
Since Amtrak is so young, they never got the
opportunity to build their own tracks.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.620
The Northeast corridor—which is absolutely
perfectly shaped to have a high speed rail

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.871
network with five major urban centers located
on a straight line—built up it’s rail

00:04:53.871 --> 00:04:58.479
system in the 1800’s and the railroad had
such an impact that towns and people flocked

00:04:58.479 --> 00:04:59.780
to the area around it.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.479
For that reason, this area is incredibly densely
populated and there truly is no open space

00:05:04.479 --> 00:05:05.900
between the cities.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.620
Consequently, it would be unbelievably expensive
to raze a bunch of houses and build a new,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.410
straighter route of high speed tracks from
DC to Boston.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:18.330
Amtrak says that it would cost an estimated
$151 billion dollars to build tracks up the

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.870
spec of France’s high speed rail network
in the Northeast corridor.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.770
Since the Northeast Regional, the train running
between DC, Baltimore, Philadelphia, New York,

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.370
and Boston, is one of the few routes that
makes money, there’s little incentive for

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:32.639
Amtrak to sink a lot of funds into upgrading
the tracks.

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:36.639
Additionally, American cities just aren’t
built like many European cities.

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:41.320
With population densities averaging lower
than 15,000 people per square mile, cities

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.070
in the United States are far less walkable
than their European counterparts which can

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:48.350
have as many as 55,000 people per square mile.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:53.259
Due to their ancient roots, European cities
naturally developed compact urban cores since

00:05:53.259 --> 00:05:56.760
for all but the rich there was no option but
to walk everywhere.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.280
Given that, it’s much easier to walk to
your destination from a train station in a

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.850
European city than it is in an American city.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.330
It’s believed that since most Americans
have to take another form of transport to

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:10.259
get to their destination after taking the
train in America, they see the train as not

00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:14.240
that much more convenient than the plane where
you also have to take another form of transport

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.479
to get to your final destination.

00:06:16.479 --> 00:06:17.479
So what’s the solution?

00:06:17.479 --> 00:06:19.110
How should America fix it’s rails?

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:23.110
Well, unfortunately, we’ll probably never
get a big network of fancy high-speed trains

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:24.310
like in France or Germany.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:28.492
There are dozens of plans in the US to build
high-speed rail lines, however few if any

00:06:28.492 --> 00:06:30.470
of them will likely come to fruition.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.270
There is a high-speed rail line currently
being built between Miami and Orlando by a

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:39.130
private company called All Aboard Florida,
however, with a top speed of 125 miles per

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:43.000
hour, the service will only be slightly faster
than driving due to speed restrictions on

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.310
many parts of the route.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:48.039
The state of California is also building a
high-speed rail line between San Francisco

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:52.690
and Anaheim with a estimated transit time
of 2 hours and 40 minutes which would be less

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.220
than half the driving time between the two
cities.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:59.460
Despite construction already beginning, phase
1 of the project isn’t estimated to be completed

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.060
until 2029 and public support is diminishing.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.600
Many have proposed that America shouldn’t
be concerned with building a flashy high-speed

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:07.600
network.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.430
Amtrak’s 151 billion dollar proposal for
a true high-speed northeast corridor system

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:19.280
divides down to $320 million dollars per mile
or $60,000 per foot of high speed track.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.949
What would be far more efficient would be
to upgrade current track to allow trains to

00:07:22.949 --> 00:07:24.780
operate at their top speeds.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:29.919
On the Northeast Regional route, trains reach
a top speed of 125mph briefly, and if they

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:33.385
operated at that speed for all of the DC to
New York leg, the trip from DC to New York

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:35.690
would take only slightly longer than two hours.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.690
Small improvements can cut minutes from the
journey times which can add up to hours.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:43.789
Unfortunately, Amtrak is stuck in a rut where
they have no money to improve anything, which

00:07:43.789 --> 00:07:47.400
causes low ridership, which worsens the problem
of no money.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:52.349
SNCF in France is so great because taxpayers
pay for about half of the operating cost of

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:56.620
every journey, while Amtrak is designed to
be a for-profit yet government subsidized

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:57.820
corporation.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:02.270
Right now, Amtrak is kinda like the neglected
little brother in the US transit family who

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:06.539
doesn’t get any money, and until that changes,
we’re still going to have our slow, expensive

00:08:06.539 --> 00:08:08.210
trains.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.229
Thank you for watching!

